  "H2_verdict": {
    "criterion": "N_res_snr = #{n : sigma_n * nu_0 > 1} on the noise-whitened operator, with nu_0 the total tephra mass above.  This is the criterion to compare across k: stacking another class adds rows, so A^T A only grows and the count cannot decrease.  The relative-spectrum count sigma_n/sigma_1 > delta is ALSO reported, but it is not comparable across k -- adding a high-amplitude class raises sigma_1 and can lower the ratio, which is why k=4 scores below k=3 on it.  That is an artefact of the criterion, not a loss of information.",
    "n_res_one_class": 10,
    "n_res_four_classes": 19,
    "n_res_picard_one_class": 2,
    "n_res_picard_four_classes": 2,
    "picard_criterion": "Optimal truncation index: where the solution coefficients |u_n^T d| / sigma_n stop falling and start being amplified by the division.  Data-driven -- it needs no assumed noise level -- but it reads the noise off the data, so it is an optimistic companion to the SNR count rather than a replacement.",
    "statement": "At the nugget noise level (sigma_log = 0.70) the benchmark's single 250-500 um fraction resolves 10 modes of nu(Q); inverting all four fractions jointly resolves 19, a gain of 9.  Counting instead the modes whose data projection exceeds one noise standard deviation gives 2 and 2.  The singular values fall geometrically by a factor 0.65 per mode, so resolution grows only logarithmically in signal-to-noise: halving the noise buys 1.6 of a mode.  Polydispersity is the cheaper lever, and it is the one the data already contain."
  },
